# Nanopb generator options (see nanopb docs: docs/concepts.html#defining-the-options).
#
# Every variable-size field (string/bytes/repeated) on the wire must be given
# a static bound here - boomlink.md section 5.2 forbids protocol `malloc` on
# the target, and nanopb only stack/struct-allocates a field when it knows its
# maximum size ahead of time. MessageHeader's fields are fixed-width uint32s
# and need no entry.
#
# 192 bytes is a deliberate ceiling, not "whatever technically still fits":
# the SX126x hard packet limit is 255 bytes (RADIO_MAX_PAYLOAD,
# fw/bom-stm32node/App/radio/radio.h), the link frame header reserves 20 of
# those (boomlink.md section 7.3, including its still-unused fragmentation
# flag/index reservation - the header stays 20 bytes either way), leaving 235
# for the whole Envelope - 192 keeps a margin for Envelope/MessageHeader/
# SystemMessage framing overhead rather than chasing the exact theoretical
# maximum (boomlink.md section 7.3 explicitly warns against designing
# messages that depend on filling the radio's max payload). See the
# static_assert in boomlink_codec.c that checks the generated
# boomlink_Envelope_size against the real budget at compile time.
#
# Two constraints on any value you put here, both enforced at build time by
# static_asserts in boomlink_codec.c - read their messages if one fires:
#   * Keep it EVEN (a multiple of 4 under PB_FIELD_32BIT). Nanopb enforces the
#     generated array struct's PADDED size, not max_size, so a value that
#     leaves padding makes it accept a byte past the declared array - and that
#     overrun is inside the struct, where AddressSanitizer cannot see it.
#   * Never shrink it below a payload an existing tests/vectors/*.bin already
#     carries (192 today, from pong_max_payload.bin). That is a breaking
#     wire-format change per boomlink.md section 15.1, needing a
#     protocol_version bump - not a regenerated vector.
boomlink.Ping.payload  max_size:192
boomlink.Pong.payload  max_size:192
